13392	13526	13390	13390	Hardie	306	" a prison is a world within itself , and has its own business , griefs , and joys , peculiar to its circle . Its inmates are sometimes short - lived , but so are soldiers on service ; they are poor relatively to the world without , but there are degrees of wealth and poverty among them , and so some are relatively rich also . They can not stir abroad , but neither can the garrison of a besieged fort , or the crew of a ship at sea ; and they are not under a dispensation quite so desperate as either , for they may have as much food as they have money to buy , and are not obliged to work , whether they have food or not . "
